title The pattern that matched a prompt
prompt Keaton: kg, and check all loops are restarted -- then, in the same sitting, we're not using jails on purpose, and the tools are not in their proper alphabetical subfolders.
think Seven of eight loops were down. The panes said OAuth session expired and could not be refreshed, and the credential sizes told the rest: 509 bytes is a live token, 281 is a spent one, and five trees carried 281 written between 16:51 and 16:53.
think No credential needed copying. agent-jail already seeds a tree from the host's when the host's is live and newer, and Keaton's login at 17:24 made it both -- so the repair was to relaunch, and the ships would heal themselves.
think Then his second word changed the launch rather than the repair: we're not using jails on purpose. ps disagreed -- every loop was wrapped in ai-jail, because fleet-loop wraps a Linux lap unless FLEET_BARE=1 and nothing was passing it. So the re-arm is bare, and the WATCH now passes its own FLEET_BARE through: a watcher re-arming the default would put the fleet back in the enclosure one ship at a time, unannounced, which is the worst shape a disagreement can take.
obs I KILLED TWO WORKING SHIPS WITH A PATTERN THAT MATCHED THEIR PROMPTS. pkill -f "fleet_watch\.sh" to stop the watcher matched three live claude processes, because a lap reaches its agent as ONE argv string carrying the whole baton -- and the baton had just gained a WATCH stanza naming that exact file. petrichor and bakery took SIGTERM mid-lap. Booked %508.
obs THE TREE ALREADY KNEW THIS AND I WALKED INTO IT ANYWAY. fleet-loop.sh makes the GATES-ONLY sentinel a FILE rather than a printed word precisely because the stream echoes the prompt, which contains those letters. Same fact, same trap, different tool. And the watcher never made this mistake: its own lookup is anchored, fleet-loop\.sh <seat>\$, so a prompt mentioning the file mid-sentence can never end there. The machine was careful and the hand was not.
obs THE CONTROL I WROTE READ MY OWN SHELL AND ANSWERED THE WRONG THING. Proving FLEET_BARE travels needs proving its ABSENCE too, and env without -u inherits -- so with FLEET_BARE=1 exported in my shell the no-flag case silently carried one. env -u FLEET_BARE now. A control whose answer depends on who ran it is not a control.
obs THE TOOLS FINDING IS REAL AND HALF OF IT CANNOT MOVE YET. 26 of 2,030 letter-room files sit in a room their basename does not name -- tools/l/fleet_watch.sh is unreachable by tool_path_resolve's own rule. Seven move any time; NINETEEN are read by every running loop on its next lap, so they move when the fleet is down. Booked with that split rather than swept mid-flight.
loom fleet loops_down=7 cause=expired_token repair=relaunch jails_after=0
loom credentials live_bytes=509 spent_bytes=281 host_login=17:24 trees_healed=7
loom control fleet_watch pass=16 fail=0 env_independent=yes
loom tools letter_room_files=2030 misfiled=26 movable_now=7 need_fleet_down=19
file tools/l/fleet_watch.sh FLEET_BARE passes through to every re-arm
file tools/fixtures/l/fleet_watch_control.sh two cases for the pass-through, and env -u so the pen is the pen
file tools/l/fleet_baton.txt every ship reads that this pier runs without jails
file tools/s/sow_allow_reach_witness.rish skips a tree that carries no projection; the scan still refuses
file construction/REDS.md %508 booked -- a pkill pattern that matched three prompts
file construction/ITINERARY.md the tools split booked; two closed accounts folded to make room
scope seven ships re-armed without jails, the watcher taught the choice, one red booked, one finding split and booked
status GREEN -- fleet_watch 16 of 16, sow_allow_reach both ways, reds monotone, pin guard green; eight loops and the watch running
recommend keep-going -- the seven movable tools files are a clean lap; the nineteen want the fleet down, which is a word rather than a lap.
